# Shell scripts run from a checkout on Windows too (Git Bash, WSL); a CRLF checkout breaks
# them, so they keep LF whatever core.autocrlf says.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The web app is built on Windows too (`cd web && npm run build` writes the production build
# into dragon/server/static), and Vite carries the source's line endings into what it writes:
# with a CRLF checkout, a build that changed nothing still rewrites files. The sources keep
# LF, so every platform produces the same bundle byte for byte. Binary media is named so
# text detection can never touch it.
web/** text=auto eol=lf
dragon/server/static/** text=auto eol=lf
web/**/*.png binary
web/**/*.webp binary
web/**/*.mp4 binary
web/**/*.woff2 binary
web/**/*.ico binary
web/**/*.svg text eol=lf
assets/**/*.png binary
assets/**/*.mp4 binary
